Only Bernoulli designs were run, because HC0 is a unit-level interval. The results concern these two bounds and the allocations of width tried; they do not say that no better interval exists, and a design that varies $p$ across units so that the corners enter the support is not tested. A diagnostic found after the failure of the expectation for the threshold mechanism shows why $B_2$ cannot tell a smooth response from a curved one: for the zero-curvature mechanisms (a) and (f) $\hat M$ is about 0 without noise and 6.9, 26 and 112 at $d=5,20,80$ at the noise of the runs, an amplification of noise over the narrow range of $f$, so the bound is wide everywhere and grows with $d$. This is a property of the estimator and is consistent with the non-adaptivity of honest intervals \citep{armstrong2020simple}; we did not test that link. An exploratory arm run after these results replaces $\hat M$ in $B_2$ on the 585 block cells without the threshold mechanism (outside the verdict, $\hat\kappa$ not re-frozen; the infeasible arms use the truth or the noise-free column of the same draw). The median $\hat M$ is 7, 25 and 109 at $d=5,20,80$, against 7.0, 25.5 and 110.5 for a column of the same draw that is noise alone, and 4.7 at most for the true supremum of $|g''|$ over $[0,1]$ in mechanisms (c), (g), (h); which is zero for (a) and (f) and also for (b) and (e), whose outcomes are linear in the assignment, so that no curvature bound in $f$ can cover their bias, and the threshold mechanism (d) has none. With the true $M$ the interval has a mean width 0.9 times that of the oracle ($\mathrm{HC0}+|\mathrm{bias}|$) at $\kappa=0.25$ and 1.7 times at $\kappa=1$, against 8.7 and 33 for the cubic, and covers 0.824 and 0.845; the same width of inflated HC0 covers 0.825 and 0.969. At $\kappa=1$ it covers 0.942 of the 423 cells of mechanisms with a finite true $M$ (2.2 times the oracle width) and 0.591 of the 162 cells of (b) and (e) (0.61 times). A cubic with the noise removed (the same fit on the treatment part alone) and a cubic less its noise level give 0.857 and 0.794 at $\kappa=0.25$ at 1.15 and 0.93 times the oracle width. So the width of $B_2$ is mostly the cost of estimating $M$ from noisy outcomes, and the narrow versions do not cover where the bias is not curvature in $f$; they are not feasible, as they use the truth or a noise-only column.

